request: In the watchlist, show movies I haven't watched yet before the ones I've watched.

governor turn 1: plan -> watchlist  (The request is a display-order change for the watchlist view. The watchlist component's `movies` signal is the single source of the rendered list order, and it already receives `watched` flags from the store, so only that unit needs to change. No exported signatures change (movies stays Signal<WatchItem[]>), and the store's contract is left untouched so other consumers are unaffected.)
round 1: owners watchlist=ok; oracle RED
governor turn 2: plan -> watchlist  (tsc is clean; the 4 failures are (a) my own acceptance test, which never provided the router the Watchlist template needs (NG0201 ActivatedRoute) — fixed by adding provideRouter([]) and a store-integrity pin — and (b) two pre-existing watchlist toggle tests that broke after the reordering change. Re-instruct watchlist to keep click-to-toggle working (no store-array mutation, no double-toggle, id tracking) without editing their tests, and to submit a change request only if those tests pin the old ordering.)
round 2: owners watchlist=rejected; oracle RED
!! GOVERNOR ISOLATION LEAK: 1 source line(s) in governor prompt
governor turn 3: plan -> watchlist  (The watchlist component already implements the requested unwatched-before-watched ordering (tsc is clean; my acceptance tests pass and pin the behaviour, including that the store's own order is untouched). The only two red tests fail purely on a positional assumption — that the just-watched movie stays in row 0 — which is exactly the behaviour this request overturns. The owner confirmed the component toggles exactly once per click, copies without mutating the store array, and tracks rows by id. I therefore approve the two minimal test edits the owner proposed: locate the clicked movie by title instead of list position, while still asserting that the click toggles that movie's watched state.)
round 3: owners watchlist=ok; oracle GREEN
governor turn 4: accept  (Oracle is green (tsc clean, 32/32 tests passing) and the watchlist acceptance tests confirm unwatched movies are ordered before watched ones, both initially and after toggling, without mutating the store. The request is satisfied.)

